---
/**
 * DIWALI KE SHOR MEIN, ON THE MOSAIC BED
 *
 * 2026-09-23. A draft of the work page with the home page's smoked mosaic
 * behind it, built to be judged against the About movements -- which is
 * where that background is seen at rest, at full smoke and full haze.
 *
 * WHAT THE BACKGROUND ACTUALLY IS, in three layers rather than one:
 *   .ms__ground  fixed, inset 0, z-index 0, overflow hidden -- the window;
 *   .ms__bed     inset -10% inside it, a 6 x 5 grid of thirty stills, and
 *                the element that carries BOTH the grade and the blur;
 *   .sm          fixed, z-index 1, the ground colour at 55 per cent -- the
 *                smoke, which is what stops the bed competing with type.
 *
 * THE GRADE IS ON THE BED, NOT THE TILE, which is the home page's own
 * arrangement and worth copying rather than reinventing:
 * brightness(0.30) saturate(0.7) contrast(1.05) as one filter on one
 * element, instead of thirty. The one exception is the tile BED_GRADE
 * names -- contrast composes multiplicatively, so 1.05 on the parent and
 * 1/1.05 on that tile resolves to 1.0 for it alone.
 *
 * THE BLUR IS BEHIND :root[data-blur] AND THAT IS NOT DECORATION. On the
 * home page an attribute is set only after a device probe passes, because a
 * 7px blur over a bed inset -10% is 1.2x the viewport in each axis -- 8.55
 * megapixels on a 1512x982 MacBook. The failure direction is no blur, which
 * is a state the page already looks right in. This draft sets the attribute
 * from a three-line script that respects prefers-reduced-motion; IF THIS IS
 * EVER APPLIED, PORT THE REAL PROBE from index.astro rather than this.
 *
 * SMOKE AND HAZE ARE STATIC HERE, at --smoke: 1 and --haze: 7px. On the
 * home page both are animated from 0 across the first viewport of scroll,
 * because the opening has to be seen clean before the bed arrives. A work
 * page has no opening to protect, and the brief was to match the About
 * section -- which is the END of that animation, not its journey.
 *
 * THE CONTENT HAD TO BE LIFTED, and this is the part that is easy to get
 * wrong. Everything on the work page is position: static, and a fixed
 * element at z-index 0 paints in step 6 of the painting order while
 * non-positioned inline content paints in step 5 -- so the bed would have
 * covered the text rather than sat behind it. The home page solves this
 * with .ov, .mvt { position: relative; z-index: 2 }; the same lift is given
 * here to .sc, the next-work link and the colophon. Measured before
 * building: html and body are the only opaque surfaces on the page, both
 * at --ground, so nothing else needed clearing out of the way.
 */
/**
 * THE WORK PAGE.
 *
 * Promoted from preview draft XXVI "Ghost" — XXIV's discipline with the
 * ghost folios: no structural rules, space doing the dividing, one drawn rule
 * per section that scales in as the section arrives, the statement assembling
 * word by word on scroll, and each section carrying its own number set
 * enormous and almost invisible behind the heading.
 *
 * WHAT THE DRAFT COULD ASSUME AND THIS CANNOT. XXVI was written against one
 * work — Aasha — which has a YouTube film, a quote and a festival record.
 * Across the catalogue only 7 of 16 works have a video, 12 have a quote and
 * 8 have awards, and the openings run video, composition, still and audio.
 * So the draft's single VideoFacade becomes a branch over `opening`, and
 * every section that depends on an optional field is guarded. A page that
 * printed "The film" over a record, or asserted a youtube id that is not
 * there, would break nine of sixteen pages.
 *
 * THE OPENING IS LABELLED "THE WORK", NOT "THE FILM". The draft could say
 * film because its subject was one; here the same section holds a film, a set
 * of films, a still or a record, and only one label is true of all four.
 * Audio openings say "Listen", which is true of both of them.
 *
 * ALL FOUR LABELLED SECTIONS ALWAYS RENDER — every work has an opening, a
 * description, frames and credits — so the page's shape is the same on all
 * sixteen. The big ghosted section numerals that used to sit behind each
 * heading have been removed.
 *
 * THE MENU IS THE HOME PAGE'S, not Base's running head. Work pages carried
 * RunHead until now, which meant leaving a work put you under a different
 * piece of furniture than the one you left — two menus for one site. They
 * mount OpNav instead, in the same "bracket" variant the home page uses, and
 * RunHead is hidden here exactly as it is there.
 *
 * IT IS MOUNTED WITH base="/". OpNav's four items are #top / #works / #about /
 * #contact, which are sections of the one-page home and are nothing at all
 * beside a work — a bare "#works" here is a link to the top of this page. The
 * base makes them "/#works", so the menu leaves the work and lands on the home
 * page at the right section. This is the reason work pages had no menu before,
 * and it is now handled in the component rather than avoided.
 *
 * NOTHING IS INVENTED: every field is read from the work's own markdown.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import OpNav from '../../components/OpNav.astro';
import VideoFacade from '../../components/VideoFacade.astro';
import FilmPlayer from '../../components/FilmPlayer.astro';
import AudioPlayer from '../../components/AudioPlayer.astro';
import NextWork from '../../components/NextWork.astro';
import Colophon from '../../components/Colophon.astro';
import { framesFor, cropFor } from '../../lib/frames';
import { parseAwards } from '../../lib/awards';
import { listFilms } from '../../lib/assets';
import { order, RATIO_NUM } from '../../data/composition';
import Still from '../../components/Still.astro';
import { BED_SWAPS, BED_CROP, BED_GRADE, BED_TILES, BED_CELLS } from '../../lib/bed';

/* The route's getStaticPaths is replaced by a single named work: a preview
   is one page, not a set. `next` is still taken from the composition order
   so the foot of the page behaves exactly as it does live. */
const SLUG = 'diwali-ke-shor-mein';
const works = await getCollection('works');
const bySlug = new Map(works.map((w) => [w.id, w]));
const sequence = order.filter((s) => bySlug.has(s));
const work = bySlug.get(SLUG)!;
const next = bySlug.get(sequence[(sequence.indexOf(SLUG) + 1) % sequence.length])!;

/* THE BED'S CAST, exactly as index.astro builds it: the composition order
   with BED_SWAPS applied, cycled to thirty, then BED_CELLS overriding the
   cells that name their own work and file. */
const bedCast = sequence.map((s) => bySlug.get(BED_SWAPS[s] ?? s)!);
const GROUND_TILES = Array.from({ length: BED_TILES }, (_, i) => {
  const pick = BED_CELLS[i];
  if (pick) return { w: bySlug.get(pick.slug)!, frames: [pick.frame], set: true };
  const w = bedCast[i % bedCast.length];
  return { w, frames: w.data.frames, set: false };
});

const d = work.data;

const images = framesFor(work.id, d.frames);
const poster = images[0];
const AR = d.ratio.replace(':', ' / ');

/* THE ROLE'S PARTS, FROM THE ONE STRING. Split on "/" only, so a role that
   carries a qualifier behind a middot — "Composer · All six episodes" on Book
   My Yamraj, "Composer · Sole composer, theatrical" on Diwali — comes through
   as ONE part and keeps its own punctuation. That is the point: those middots
   join a role to its scope, they do not divide two roles, and a rule that
   split on them would set "Composer" and "All six episodes" as if they were
   equal credits.

   filter(Boolean) so a trailing slash in some future markdown cannot produce
   an empty part and a hairline with nothing after it. */
const ROLES = d.role.split('/').map((r) => r.trim()).filter(Boolean);

/* Films listed in the markdown win; otherwise take whatever is in the folder. */
const films = d.media.films.length ? d.media.films : listFilms(work.id);

/* HOW WIDE ONE FILM WANTS TO BE, from its own shape. Given the full measure a
   vertical reel becomes enormous: measured on Book My Yamraj, each 9:16 film
   rendered 1126x2040 — 2.83 screens EACH, six of them making 17.1 screens of
   film inside a 23-screen page. The fix is not a smaller max-width but a grid
   whose track is sized to the aspect the films actually are, so a portrait
   reel sits three or four across and a landscape one sits two.

   Read off the films rather than assumed: a page mixing shapes gets the
   middle value instead of a track that suits only half of them. */
/* HOW WIDE ONE STILL WANTS TO BE, from the work's own shape. The grid was a
   fixed three columns, which is right for a landscape frame and wrong for a
   portrait one: at 9:16 those three columns gave cells 340 wide and 604 tall,
   so nine stills ran to three rows of very tall pictures.

   The track is sized so a ROW comes out about the same height whatever the
   aspect — 300px times the aspect — then capped at 340, which is exactly what
   three columns already gave. A landscape work therefore keeps its three
   columns and nothing about it changes; only shapes taller than about 1.13:1
   gain columns and shrink. The floor stops a very tall frame collapsing into
   a thumbnail. */
const STILL_TRACK = `${Math.round(Math.min(340, Math.max(160, 300 * (RATIO_NUM[d.ratio] ?? 1.6))))}px`;

const filmRatios = new Set(films.map((f) => f.ratio));
const FILM_TRACK =
  filmRatios.size === 1 && filmRatios.has('16:9') ? '420px'
  : filmRatios.size === 1 && filmRatios.has('1:1') ? '300px'
  : filmRatios.size === 1 && filmRatios.has('9:16') ? '240px'
  : '300px';

/* A film shows wherever there is one to show, rather than only where the
   opening is declared `video`. Diwali Ke Shor Mein is a `still` opening — the
   feature itself is not embeddable and lives behind its external link — but it
   has a trailer, and a moving image is a better opening than a frozen frame.
   No other page changes: every work that has a youtube id is already declared
   `video`. */
const isVideo = !!d.media.youtube || !!d.media.vimeo || !!d.media.instagram;
const isAudio = d.opening === 'audio';
/* A `composition` opening only counts as one if it has films to show.
   bloody-bubbly is declared `composition` and has no films listed and none in
   /public, so the section rendered a heading with nothing under it — measured
   on the built page. Anything that cannot show its declared medium falls
   through to the poster, which every work has. */
const hasFilms = d.opening === 'composition' && films.length > 0;
/* Everything that is not one of the above shows its poster — which covers
   `still`, `typographic`, a `video` whose id is missing, and a `composition`
   whose films have not arrived yet. */
const OPEN_LABEL = isAudio ? 'Listen' : 'The work';

/* A work with an external watch block already carries that link prominently;
   repeating it under the credits would be the same link twice on one page. */
const creditLinks = d.links.filter((l) => l.url !== d.external?.url);

/* WHERE THE EXTERNAL LINK GOES DEPENDS ON WHETHER THE PAGE CAN PLAY THE WORK.
   Five works carry one: Diwali (IMDb), Taskaree (Netflix), Special Ops
   (Hotstar) and the two songs (Spotify).

   A page with a facade or a set of films already OFFERS the work — there is
   something to press — so the outside link is a second route and belongs with
   the credits, in the same section Aasha's YouTube link sits in. That is
   Diwali, whose Vimeo trailer gives it a window even though its opening is
   declared `still`.

   A page whose opening is a bare STILL has no window at all: that frame is the
   work as far as the page is concerned, and the outside link is the only way
   to watch it. So it goes ON the image, where the facade puts its own Watch
   button, and the image is given the film's width to match. That is Taskaree
   and Special Ops.

   THE SONGS WERE THE EXCEPTION AND ARE NOT ANY MORE, on 2026-09-06. Their
   opening is `audio`, so they render a sleeve and a player rather than a
   still, and "Listen on Spotify" filed underneath was judged the right shape
   for a record. Ugam asked for the sleeve to carry the button the way a still
   does, and for the link to appear in the credits as well — so it is now in
   both places on those two pages, and the .ext block that used to hold it is
   gone. Nothing else reached that block; the songs were its only users.

   `hasWindow` used to live here — isVideo || hasFilms — and is gone with the
   rule that used it: the songs now take the credits link without having a
   window, so the question that const asked is no longer the one being
   decided. */
const isStill = !isVideo && !hasFilms && !isAudio;
/* WHERE THE OUTSIDE LINK IS FILED, on 2026-09-06.

   Every one of the five works that has an `external` also lists that same URL
   in `links`, so `creditLinks` is empty for all five and the fallback below is
   what actually fires. The test used to be `hasWindow` — does the page have
   something you can press to play the work — which put the link in the credits
   for Diwali and nowhere for the songs, because an audio opening is not a
   window in that sense.

   The songs now carry it in BOTH places on Ugam's instruction: on the sleeve
   and again in the credits. So the test is no longer about windows. It is:
   the credits carry the outside link unless the page is a BARE STILL, where
   the button sits on the frame and the credits follow close enough underneath
   that repeating it would be the same link twice with nothing in between.

   Taskaree and Special Ops are the bare stills and are unchanged. Aladdin has
   no external at all. Diwali is unchanged. */
const watchLinks =
  creditLinks.length > 0
    ? creditLinks
    : d.external && !isStill
      ? [{ label: d.external.label, url: d.external.url }]
      : [];

/* Split on spaces only. The words keep their own punctuation, so nothing has
   to be re-inserted and the joined text is identical to the source. */
const QW = (d.quote ?? '').split(' ').filter(Boolean);
/* A · STANDFIRST, applied 2026-09-07. The first sentence is the standfirst
   and the rest is the paragraph, so both halves have to be split out of the
   one string — and both are then split again into WORDS, because the reading
   light writes a --v per word and it must keep running over both.

   THE SPLIT IS ON A FULL STOP FOLLOWED BY WHITESPACE. It would also split an
   abbreviation, and no description in the catalogue contains one — checked
   across all sixteen, which give between three and four sentences each. If a
   description ever opens "Dir. Rohan Bakshi shot..." this is the line that
   breaks, and it fails softly: the lede would simply be short.

   THE ONE-SENTENCE CASE IS HANDLED rather than assumed away. LEDE falls back
   to the whole description and REST comes out empty, in which case the second
   paragraph is not rendered at all rather than rendered blank. */
const SENT = d.description.trim().split(/(?<=\.)\s+/).filter(Boolean);
const LEDE = SENT[0] ?? d.description.trim();
const REST = SENT.slice(1).join(' ');
/* NO WORD ARRAYS ANY MORE. A1 · Lead and lag animates the two BLOCKS, so the
   description is no longer split into words, no longer wrapped in 69 spans,
   and no longer carries a per-word seed in an inline style. The statement
   above still is and still does — its rule is unchanged. */


/* THE FIELD TAKES TWO SHAPES AND THEY ARE SET DIFFERENTLY.
   Counts — "7 Official Selections · 10 Nominations · 6 Wins" — are figures, and
   Aasha and Broken Reflections keep the ruled band of big numerals below. Every
   other awarded work carries citations instead: an honour and the body that
   gave it, one entry per award. Those now set as an index.

   The test is unchanged, so which work gets which treatment is unchanged too:
   if every dot-separated part opens with a figure it is a tally, otherwise it
   is citations. Measured against the catalogue, that is Aasha and Broken
   Reflections on one side and the six citation works on the other.

   HAS_AWARDS replaces the old joined string, which existed only to be split
   apart again — see src/lib/awards.ts for why that join was destructive. */
const HAS_AWARDS = d.awards.length > 0;
const AW_PARTS = d.awards.map((a) => a.text).join(' \u00b7 ').split('\u00b7').map((p) => p.trim()).filter(Boolean);
const AW_MATCH = AW_PARTS.map((p) => p.match(/^(\d+)\s+(.+)$/));
const TALLY = AW_MATCH.length && AW_MATCH.every(Boolean)
  ? AW_MATCH.map((m) => ({ n: m![1], k: m![2] }))
  : null;
/* Each award kept whole, and split on its FIRST dot only. */
const CITES = TALLY ? [] : parseAwards(d.awards);

/* EVERY CREDIT THE SCHEMA ALLOWS, IN ONE ORDERED LIST. This read only
   `director` and `artist` and silently dropped the other four, which is not a
   layout choice — the information simply never reached the page. Eleven of the
   sixteen works were losing a credit: `client` on Aladdin, Dinshaw's and Bloody
   Bubbly, `studio` on Book My Yamraj, Moholla, Mumbai Standard Time, My Red
   Crayon, Roar and The Shiva Trilogy, and `with` on Taskaree and Special Ops —
   the last two being collaborators named in the markdown and credited nowhere.

   Driven off a list rather than a chain of appends so a key added to
   content.config.ts cannot be forgotten here; an unhandled key now shows up as
   a missing label rather than as silence. The order and the wording follow
   CreditBlock.astro, which had the full map all along and is not mounted by
   anything — except that `director` stays "Director" rather than becoming
   "Direction", because that is what these pages already say. */
const CREDIT_ORDER: [keyof typeof d.credits, string][] = [
  ['director', 'Director'],
  ['client', 'Client'],
  ['studio', 'Studio'],
  ['production', 'Production'],
  ['artist', 'Artist'],
  ['with', 'Collaboration'],
];

/* NO SLASH IN THE COLOPHON EITHER, from 2026-09-06. Every "/" in a VALUE
   becomes a middot, which is the mark the catalogue already uses inside a
   single credit — "Neeraj Pandey · Shivam Nair", "Original score · In-house
   mix". So the rule the page now follows end to end is: a middot separates
   ITEMS WITHIN one category, and nothing but space separates one category
   from the next.

   IT IS A RULE OVER VALUES, NOT A LIST OF WORKS, so it also catches the two
   Recognition rows that carry one — "Best AI Film · Waves / IFFI" on My Red
   Crayon and "Official Selection · Waves / IFFI" on Moholla — which a rule
   written only for `role` would have missed. Eleven values in the catalogue
   contain a slash and all eleven are lists.

   WHAT WOULD BREAK IT: a value where "/" is not a separator — a date written
   9/16, a ratio, a fraction. There is none today, and this is the note to
   read if one is added. The markdown is untouched, as always. */
const dots = (v: string) => v.split('/').map((p) => p.trim()).filter(Boolean).join(' · ');

const FACTS: { k: string; v: string }[] = [
  { k: 'Role', v: dots(d.role) },
  ...CREDIT_ORDER.filter(([key]) => d.credits[key]).map(([key, label]) => ({
    k: label,
    v: dots(d.credits[key]!),
  })),
  ...d.meta.map((m) => ({ k: m.label, v: dots(m.value) })),
];
---

<Base title={`${d.title} — Ugam Khetani`} description={d.description}>
  {/* The bed and its smoke, copied from index.astro's markup unchanged. */}
  <div class="ms__ground" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="ms__bed">
      {GROUND_TILES.map((c) => (
        <span
          class="ms__tile"
          class:list={[{ 'ms__tile--crop': !!BED_CROP[c.w.id] }]}
          style={[
            BED_CROP[c.w.id] &&
              `--crop-s:${BED_CROP[c.w.id].scale};--crop-y:${BED_CROP[c.w.id].shiftY}%`,
            !c.set && BED_GRADE[c.w.id] && `--bed-k:${BED_GRADE[c.w.id].contrast}`,
          ]
            .filter(Boolean)
            .join(';') || undefined}
        >
          <Still slug={c.w.id} title={c.w.data.title} year={c.w.data.year}
            category={c.w.data.category} ratio={c.w.data.ratio}
            frames={c.frames} alt={c.w.data.alt}
            sizes="20vw" eager={false} max={1} free={true} />
        </span>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="sm" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <OpNav variant="bracket" base="/" />

  <article class="sc" data-sc style={[`--ar:${AR}`, cropFor(work.id)].filter(Boolean).join(';')}>
    {/* ── HEAD ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <header class="hd">
      {/* J1 · OVERHEAD, applied 2026-09-06. What the work IS goes above the
          title; what UGAM DID goes below it. The single line that used to
          carry both — "Short Film · 2025 · Composer · Dir. Sameer
          Talawdekar" — filed his credit among the work's facts as though it
          were one more of them.

          THE DIRECTOR IS NOT ON THIS LINE ANY MORE, removed 2026-09-06. It
          sat here for one day. Six works carried one and two named several —
          Taskaree names three, which made its top line 517px of ink — so the
          line that was meant to say what the work IS was mostly saying who
          else made it. THE CREDIT IS NOT LOST: `director` is the first row of
          the colophon below, on all six, and Diwali names Rohan Bakshi twice
          there because its markdown also carries a Direction row in `meta`.

          THE YEAR CAN BE REPLACED, and on exactly one work it is. Diwali sets
          `overline: "Theatrical"` and reads "Feature Film · Theatrical",
          because that film's release matters more than its date. Its year is
          still on the page — a Year row in `meta`, set in the colophon — and
          content.config.ts warns that a work setting an overline WITHOUT such
          a row would lose its year altogether.

          THE FOLIO IS GONE. "001 / 16" was the one element that admitted the
          other fifteen works existed; without it each work is a page unto
          itself. numberFor() and total are no longer imported here — an
          import kept for a deleted element is how a page ends up with a data
          dependency nobody can explain. numberFor() and total are still
          exported from composition.ts; /contents was their last reader and
          it was removed on 2026-09-07, so nothing on the site calls them
          today. */}
      <p class="mono hd__over">{d.category} · {d.overline ?? d.year}</p>
      <h1 class="hd__t" data-reveal>
        <span class="line"><span class="line__in">{d.title}</span></span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mono hd__meta">
        <span class="hd__roles">
          {ROLES.map((r, i) => (<>{i > 0 && <i class="hd__bar" aria-hidden="true"></i>}<span>{r}</span></>))}
        </span>
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* ── THE STATEMENT, ASSEMBLED. Twelve of sixteen works have one. ── */}
    {QW.length > 0 && (
      <section class="q" aria-label="Statement">
        {/* One line, deliberately: a multiline fragment emits its own
            indentation as text, which lands on top of the explicit space and
            doubles it. */}
        <p class="q__t">{QW.map((w, i) => (<><span class="w" data-w>{w}</span>{i < QW.length - 1 ? ' ' : ''}</>))}</p>
      </section>
    )}

    {/* ── 01 · THE WORK. One section, four kinds of media. ────── */}
    <section class="blk" aria-label={OPEN_LABEL} data-reveal>

      {isVideo && (
        <VideoFacade youtube={d.media.youtube} vimeo={d.media.vimeo} instagram={d.media.instagram} title={d.title} poster={poster} alt={d.alt} eager />
      )}

      {hasFilms && (
        <div class="films" style={`--fw:${FILM_TRACK}`}>
          {films.map((f, i) => (
            <FilmPlayer
              slug={work.id}
              src={f.src}
              label={f.label}
              href={f.href}
              poster={f.poster}
              ratio={f.ratio}
              fallbackPoster={images[i % images.length]}
              alt={`${d.alt} — ${f.label}`}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      )}

      {isAudio && (
        <div class="cover">
          {/* SIZES IS DELIBERATELY THE CEILING, NOT THE TYPICAL WIDTH. The sleeve
            renders 576px at 1280x800 and 778 at 1920x1080, and both sources are
            square and small — Ve Maahi 1024, Mere Ho 1254 — so on a 2x display
            the largest candidate is the only one that is not a visible upscale.
            Declared at 720 the browser picked the 720 candidate and rendered a
            576px box at 1.6x; declared at the ceiling it picks 1024 and the
            worst case is 1.13x. It costs one 1024px square on a page that
            already streams audio. */}
          <Image src={poster} alt={d.alt} widths={[512, 768, 1024]} sizes="(max-width: 640px) 92vw, 1024px" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
          {d.external && (
            <a class="still__go" href={d.external.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <span class="mono">{d.external.label}</span>
              <span class="still__arw" aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</span>
            </a>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
      {isAudio && d.media.audio.map((a) => <AudioPlayer slug={work.id} src={a.src} title={a.title} />)}

      {!isVideo && !hasFilms && !isAudio && (
        <div class="still">
          <Image src={poster} alt={d.alt} widths={[800, 1400, 2000]} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 78vw" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
          {d.external && (
            <a class="still__go" href={d.external.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              <span class="mono">{d.external.label}</span>
              <span class="still__arw" aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</span>
            </a>
          )}
        </div>
      )}

    </section>

    {/* ── 02 · THE WRITING, ASSEMBLED ─────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="blk" aria-label="About the work">
      {/* ONE CLOCK ON THE WRAPPER, not one per block and not one per word.
          A1 fixes the relationship between the standfirst and the paragraph,
          so both have to read the same --v; a [data-v] on each block would
          make the lag depend on where each happened to sit. */}
      <div class="body" data-v>
        {/* --n is the standfirst's own length in characters. Everything about
            how it sets is derived from that number; see the CSS. */}
        <p class="body__lede" style={`--n:${LEDE.length}`}>{LEDE}</p>
        {REST && <p class="body__rest">{REST}</p>}
      </div>

      {/* Eight of sixteen works carry a record — two as counts, six as citations. */}
      {HAS_AWARDS && (TALLY ? (
        <div class="tally" aria-label="Recognition" data-reveal>
          <dl class="tally__row">
            {TALLY.map((t) => (
              <div class="tally__cell">
                <dt class="tally__n">{t.n}</dt>
                <dd class="mono tally__k">{t.k}</dd>
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </div>
      ) : (
        <div class="rec" aria-label="Recognition">
          <ol class="rec__list">
            {CITES.map((c, i) => (
              <li class="rec__row" data-v>
                <span class="mono rec__n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                <span class="rec__body">
                  <span class="mono rec__cit">
                    {c.star && <span class="rec__star" aria-hidden="true">★</span>}
                    {c.citation}
                  </span>
                  {c.body && <span class="rec__where display">{c.body}</span>}
                </span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      ))}
    </section>

    {/* ── 03 · THE FRAMES ─────────────────────────────────────── */}
    <section class="blk" aria-label="Stills">
      <div class="grid" style={`--sw:${STILL_TRACK}`}>
        {images.map((img, i) => (
          <figure class="cel" data-reveal style={`--i:${i % 3}`}>
            <span class="cel__win">
              <Image
                src={img}
                alt={i === 0 ? d.alt : ''}
                aria-hidden={i === 0 ? undefined : 'true'}
                widths={[420, 700, 1100]}
                sizes="(max-width: 720px) 46vw, 30vw"
                loading={i < 3 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                decoding="async"
              />
            </span>
            <figcaption class="mono cel__no">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── 04 · WATCH — lifted out of the credits ──────────────────
        It was a `.tbl__links` paragraph at the foot of the credits table, so
        the one action on the page sat inside a block of reference data. It is
        its own section now, above the credits and set at title scale.
        Seven of sixteen works carry one; the guard means the other nine
        render nothing here rather than an empty band. */}
    {watchLinks.length > 0 && (
      <section class="blk wt" aria-label="Watch">
        {watchLinks.map((l) => (
          <a class="wt__a" href={l.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-v>
            <span class="wt__t display">{l.label}</span>
            {/* TWO BOXES FOR THE ARROW, AND THE REASON IS THE TRANSITION. The
                outer one carries the scroll-driven transform and must NOT be
                transitioned, or it chases the scroll 240ms behind. The inner
                one carries the hover nudge, which must be. One property on
                two elements rather than two behaviours fighting over one. */}
            <span class="wt__arw" aria-hidden="true"><span class="wt__arw-i">&nearr;</span></span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </section>
    )}

    {/* ── 05 · CREDITS, as a colophon ─────────────────────────────
        No heading. The dl is kept and set inline rather than swapped for a
        <p>, so the markup still says "these are pairs" to a screen reader
        while reading as prose. */}
    <section class="blk cr" aria-label="Credits">
      {/* ONE CLOCK, ON THE LIST. C · Colophon fixes the relationship between
          the labels and their values, so both halves have to read the same
          --v; a [data-v] on each row would make the lag depend on where each
          pair happened to sit and would quietly turn this back into a
          per-row cascade. */}
      <dl class="cr__list" data-v>
        {FACTS.map((f) => (
          <div class="cr__row">
            <dt class="mono cr__k">{f.k}</dt>
            <dd class="cr__v">{f.v}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
    </section>
  </article>

  <NextWork
    slug={next.id}
    title={next.data.title}
    year={next.data.year}
    category={next.data.category}
    frames={next.data.frames}
    alt={next.data.alt}
  />
  <Colophon />
</Base>

<style>
  .sc { padding: clamp(96px, 15vh, 190px) var(--margin) 0; }

  /* ── HEAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hd { max-width: 1180px; margin-inline: auto; text-align: center; }
  /* The line above the title. It keeps the folio's exact position and size —
     only what it says has changed — so the distance from the menu to the
     title is what it always was. */
  .hd__over {
    margin: 0 0 clamp(24px, 4vh, 48px);
    font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--dim);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .hd__t {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 500;
    /* 7.25rem, not the 8rem this was drawn at, and the measure is why. At
       128px the longest word in the catalogue — "Reflections" — sets 807px
       against a 760px column and spilled 47px past the right edge, which is
       the one thing that would have contradicted the whole point of a narrow
       page. Measured across all sixteen titles it is the only one that
       overflows, and the ceiling that fits it is 120px; 116px leaves 29px of
       margin. Nothing else needed changing. */
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 10vw, 7.25rem);
    line-height: 0.86; letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--bone);
  }
  /* THE ROLES, DIVIDED BY A DRAWN HAIRLINE RATHER THAN BY A SLASH. A "/" is
     shorthand — it means "or", or it means a path — and neither is true of a
     credit; these are things that were all done, which in English is a list.
     A 7px rule has no baseline, no weight to match the letters and cannot be
     read as a character, which is what a separator should be.

     THE SLASH IS GONE FROM THE RENDERING, NOT FROM THE DATA. The markdown
     still says "Music / Sound / Voice Over" and must: the same string is
     set in this page's own credits list below, so editing it would move two
     places at once. It was three until /contents was removed on 2026-09-07.

     --dim, not --muted. Both lines around the title now sit at the same
     weight, so neither is the caption of the other. */
  .hd__meta {
    display: flex; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin: clamp(28px, 4.6vh, 50px) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--dim);
  }
  /* THE WRAP IS INSURANCE, NOT A FIX FOR ANYTHING IN THE CATALOGUE TODAY.
     Measured at 375, where the measure is 327: the longest role list is
     Special Ops' "Asst. Composer | Additional Production" and it inks at 287,
     so it fits on one line with 40px to spare. Nothing in the sixteen wraps.
     It is allowed to break so that a longer role added later breaks instead
     of pushing the page wide — the failure it prevents is horizontal scroll,
     which is the one thing a narrow page must never do. */
  .hd__roles { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center; row-gap: 8px; }
  .hd__bar {
    display: inline-block; width: 1px; height: 7px;
    margin-inline: clamp(10px, 1.8vw, 16px);
    background: var(--rule);
  }

  /* ── THE ASSEMBLING WORD ────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Each word carries its own --v, 0 before it is reached and 1 once it is,
     written by the clock. Everything visual is derived from that one number,
     so a word cannot be half-lifted and fully lit at the same time. */
  .w {
    display: inline-block;
    opacity: calc(0.12 + var(--v, 1) * 0.88);
    transform: translateY(calc((1 - var(--v, 1)) * 0.32em));
    will-change: opacity, transform;
  }

  /* A1 · LEAD AND LAG, applied 2026-09-07, replacing W3 · Stagger the same
     day. The standfirst rides the page's own --v and the paragraph lags on
     --v SQUARED, so the opening line is readable while the body is still
     coming up.

     IT IS THE PAGE'S OWN DEVICE, SAID TWICE. A recognition row already
     separates its figure from the festival that gave it exactly this way.
     Applying it here is not a new idea; it is the same idea at another
     scale, which is the cheapest coherence a page can buy.

     A LAG IS A SQUARE, NOT A DELAY. v * v trails v everywhere except at 0 and
     1, where both are exact — so the two blocks separate through the middle
     and land together, with no timer, nothing to keep in step, and a clean
     reverse on the way back up.

     THE DESCRIPTION IS NO LONGER SET WORD BY WORD, and that is a real
     simplification rather than a side effect: 49 to 71 spans per page, each
     with an inline custom property, are gone from every work page. The
     statement above still assembles word by word — .w and its rule are
     untouched — so the page still has a per-word moment; it now has one
     rather than two.

     THE PARAGRAPH TRAVELS FURTHER THAN THE STANDFIRST, 0.5em against 0.32,
     because it is smaller type: an em is relative to the element, so equal
     em values would have made the body move less in pixels as well as later.
     Measured at a --v of 0.5, the standfirst sits 5.568px low at 0.56 opacity
     and the paragraph 7.5px low at 0.325. */
  .body { --v2: calc(var(--v, 1) * var(--v, 1)); }
  .body__lede {
    opacity: calc(0.12 + var(--v, 1) * 0.88);
    transform: translateY(calc((1 - var(--v, 1)) * 0.32em));
    will-change: opacity, transform;
  }
  .body__rest {
    opacity: calc(0.1 + var(--v2, 1) * 0.9);
    transform: translateY(calc((1 - var(--v2, 1)) * 0.5em));
    will-change: opacity, transform;
  }

  .q {
    max-width: 1180px;
    margin: clamp(80px, 14vh, 180px) auto;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .q__t {
    margin: 0 auto; max-width: 20ch;
    font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 5.4vw, 4.4rem);
    line-height: 1.02; letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    color: var(--bone);
  }

  /* ── BLOCKS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .blk { max-width: 1180px; margin: 0 auto clamp(72px, 12vh, 160px); }
  /* The rule draws itself across the measure as the section arrives — scaled
     from the left on one transform, so it composites rather than reflowing. */

  .body { margin: 0; }

  /* THE STANDFIRST, AND WHY ITS SIZE IS DERIVED RATHER THAN SET.
     The draft was drawn against Mumbai Standard Time, whose first sentence is
     80 characters. Across the catalogue they run 55 to 166 — The Shiva
     Trilogy's shortest, Aasha's longest — a spread of three to one. At the
     draft's fixed 2.2rem over 24ch, Aasha's would have set SEVEN lines of
     35px display type, which is not a standfirst but a slab. A fixed size
     cannot serve both ends of that spread, so both the size and the measure
     are computed from --n, the sentence's own character count.

     THE MEASURE targets about three lines: n/3 characters wide, held between
     20ch so a short one cannot become a narrow ribbon and 44ch so a long one
     cannot outrun the reading width.

     THE SIZE eases down as the sentence lengthens, 0.007rem per character
     past 55, so a long first sentence reads as an emphasised opening rather
     than as a wall. The clamp floor of 1.2rem keeps it clearly above the
     paragraph beneath it at every length.

     AND IT IS CAPPED BY THE VIEWPORT AS WELL, which the first version of this
     rule forgot. The draft's size was 3vw and shrank on a phone; a rule
     derived only from character count does not, so at 375 The Shiva Trilogy's
     55-character standfirst still set 35px and ran to four lines in a 327px
     column. The min() against 1.05rem + 2.1vw binds on narrow screens and is
     inert on wide ones, where the length rule governs alone.

     Both are one expression each. There is no per-work table and no slug
     named anywhere: a work whose description is rewritten re-sets itself. */
  .body__lede {
    margin: 0 0 clamp(22px, 3.4vh, 40px);
    max-width: clamp(20ch, calc(var(--n, 90) / 3 * 1ch), 44ch);
    font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(
      1.2rem,
      min(calc(2.35rem - (var(--n, 90) - 55) * 0.007rem), calc(1.05rem + 2.1vw)),
      2.2rem
    );
    line-height: 1.12; letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    color: var(--bone); text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .body__rest {
    margin: 0; max-width: 54ch;
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 1.6vw, 1.25rem);
    line-height: 1.62; letter-spacing: -0.006em;
    color: var(--bone-dim); text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* ── RECOGNITION — X's band ─────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Three figures set as figures on a ruled band. It sits where IX's one-line
     awards caption sat, so the order is untouched — only the treatment. */
  .tally {
    margin: clamp(44px, 7vh, 90px) 0 0;
    padding-block: clamp(26px, 4.4vh, 52px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .tally__row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); margin: 0; }
  .tally__cell { text-align: center; padding-inline: clamp(8px, 2vw, 28px); }
  /* Hairlines between the cells only — a border on each would draw one down
     the outer edges too, where the band already has its own ends. */
  .tally__cell + .tally__cell { border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-soft); }
  .tally__n {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display); font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 7vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 0.82; letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    color: var(--bone); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .tally__k {
    margin: clamp(12px, 2vh, 20px) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--t-micro); letter-spacing: 0.16em;
